WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.380
It’s 2015.

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.720
We got pictures of Pluto that look like this.

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.600
Doc Brown and Marty have arrived from 1985.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.260
And it’s the 100th anniversary of general relativity!

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.780
Welcome to the Science Asylum. I am Nick Lucid.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.720
In a previous video, I gave you an overview
of what Einstein had to say about gravity

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.980
and you told me three and a half minutes wasn’t long enough.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.780
Remember, you asked for this.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.980
General Relativity says massive things will curve paths that would normally be straight lines,

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.460
but that can be really confusing if you’ve never seen anything like this before.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.300
So let’s start with a simpler question:

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:35.600
What’s relativity?

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.480
To understand that, we really need to go over some history.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:43.960
It all starts in 1632 with a crazy Italian guy named Galileo.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.080
While he was busy sticking it to the man a.k.a. the Vatican,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:50.140
he suggested, amongst other things, that all physical laws

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.579
should be the same in every inertial reference frame or IRF.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:54.740
What’s a reference frame?

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.360
I guess that does sound like some complicated science thing, but it really isn’t.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:04.440
Typically, physics uses a coordinate system or frame to refer to measurements.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.280
So your reference frame is just the coordinate system attached to your body.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.800
It’s your physical point of view.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.520
So what’s it mean for that to be inertial?

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.120
Physically, the word “inertial” just means that motion stays the same,

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.720
so an inertial reference frame is just a coordinate system that moves with a steady speed in a straight line,

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.320
but let’s give this some context.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.600
Here, we have AwkwardM standing on the ground and Chauffeur Clone driving a car.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:32.140
Both have coordinate systems with steady motion and both completely agree about what forces are involved.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:33.660
The laws of physics are the same.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.680
This has a weird consequence though. They don’t agree on who is moving.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:42.300
AwkwardM would say the car is moving, but Chauffeur Clone would say AwkwardM is moving.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.880
No no no, AwkwardM is on the ground, so she’s the one that’s sitting still, right?

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:46.680
False!

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:52.250
The ground is moving due East at 770 miles per hour since the Earth is rotating.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:56.740
The Earth also goes around the Sun at 65,000 miles per hour.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.840
I could go on, but I think you get the point.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.780
The important thing to understand here:

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.920
It’s not just that you can’t tell who’s moving and who’s sitting still.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.260
Inertial motion is relative so both AwkwardM and Chauffeur Clone are correct.

00:02:09.260 --> 00:02:12.340
All measurements of motion are correct even if they disagree.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.580
That’s relativity!

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.920
And it’s not just limited to the motion of the frames.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:21.480
If Chauffeur Clone is driving at 20 miles per hour and then throws a ball at 30 miles per hour,

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.340
AwkwardM will see the ball moving at 50 miles per hour.

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.380
But the clone only sees the ball moving at 30 miles per hour.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.240
But how does Einstein fit into all of this?

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.640
OK, this kind of relativity is known as Galilean relativity.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.940
It’s another 273 years before we get to Einstein.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:39.040
There’s way more timeline to go through.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.640
The story is important, OK?!

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.880
He solved a major problem 273 years later.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.620
Back to the timeline, crazies!

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.700
So, 1632, relativity was born

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.520
and then Pope Urban the 8th put Galileo under house arrest for the rest of his life.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:57.520
From 1665-1667, Cambridge closed due to an outbreak of bubonic plague

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.100
and a rebellious undergrad college student named Isaac Newton was sent home.

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:05.580
That gave him the free time to develop his laws of motion, calculus, some optics stuff,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.270
and his universal law of gravity; which was far from universal even then.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:14.520
He published none of it until 1687 because he didn’t want anyone else to know how to do it.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:15.800
Selfish doo-doo-head!

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:22.140
Anyway, everything was going just fine until some jerk-face named James Maxwell showed up in 1865!

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.440
He discovered that, not only was light an electromagnetic wave,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.340
but also, that this meant the speed of light was a universal constant.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.080
What what?!

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:31.860
Yeah, this is a serious problem.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:32.960
How is that a problem?

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.200
Sure. Maybe if you just think about it for a second, you’re like: Meh. Whatever.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.840
But we don’t do any of that shallow thinking here in the asylum.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.820
Asylum Crazies think deep!

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.840
Remember AwkwardM and Chauffeur Clone?

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.460
AwkwardM would say the car is moving, but Chauffeur Clone would say AwkwardM is moving.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:51.660
Remember, both are correct. That’s relativity.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.820
and it makes perfect sense if we're talking about a ball.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.700
But what happens if we try this with light?

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.840
Say you’re in a spaceship zipping past my space station at half the speed of light

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.240
and then you turn on its headlights.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.800
You would see the light move forward at the speed of light,

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.440
but do I see it move forward 1.5 times the speed of light?

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:08.280
No!

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.640
I also see it move forward at the speed of light.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:15.160
The speed of light is a universal constant, so everybody has to measure it exactly the same,

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.920
no matter where they are or what they’re doing.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.720
Houston. We have a problem.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:26.180
Galileo says “All motion is relative.” And then, 230 years later, Maxwell’s all like “Well, not for light.”

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:28.480
So now we have two competing theories of motion.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.480
Is Galileo right? or is Maxwell right? The debate raged on for four decades.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.900
Suggestions of universal reference frames,

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:38.620
measurements of the speed of light in multiple directions,

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:43.220
and then finally, in 1905, Albert Einstein showed up on the scene with 5 papers:

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.610
Measurement of the Mole, Brownian Motion,

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:50.520
Photoelectric Effect, E equals M C squared, and a crazy but brilliant suggestion for our problem:

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.180
What if Galileo and Maxwell are right?

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.920
The only way they can both be right is if we’re wrong about everything else.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:04:58.860
Completely wrong?

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.240
No no, just a little wrong.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.260
If the speed of light is the same for everyone, then nearly every other measurement must be relative:

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:08.640
Time, Distance, Length, Energy, Momentum.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.800
They must all depend on your point of view.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:16.180
We call this “Einstein’s Special Theory of Relativity” or “Special Relativity” for short

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.240
because it only works in a special case:

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.040
When you’re taking measurements in an inertial reference frame.

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:24.040
If you want to take measurements in an accelerated reference frame,

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:26.740
one that isn’t moving steadily in a straight line,

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:31.460
then we’re going to need a more “General Theory of Relativity” or “General Relativity” for short.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:32.860
But we have a minor issue.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.240
Physics isn’t built on the things that are relative. It’s built on the things that stay the same,

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.560
like the laws of physics between reference frames.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.700
We’re quickly running out of things that stay the same.

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:44.880
Einstein solved this problem too, but it took him another decade.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.720
What happens in accelerated reference frames?

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:48.980
Hmm, let’s take a look at one!

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:49.960
Are you sure you've got this?

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:51.980
Yes yes, I got this.

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:52.520
Alright.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.420
Let’s say there’s a ball hanging from a car ceiling.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.400
When the car is sitting still, the ball hangs straight up and down.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:01.800
There’s a force of gravity pulling down and a force of tension pulling up.

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.800
But if it accelerates, the ball swings toward the back of the car.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.080
AwkwardM standing outside doesn’t see anything all that strange.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:13.260
The car is accelerating forward and the extra tension is what also accelerates the ball forward,

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:14.620
so everything makes sense.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:16.060
In the car we have a problem.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:19.520
Everything in there looks like it’s sitting still. There’s no acceleration.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:23.180
So the only explanation is there must be a new force on the ball.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:24.360
So what’s the new force?

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.600
In all my merry-go-round videos, I called it a fictitious force

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.700
because it doesn’t exist from all points of view.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:32.980
It exists inside the car, but not outside the car.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:36.120
A force is just an interaction between two or more objects.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.640
The tension is between the ball and the string, and gravity is between the ball and the Earth,

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:42.260
But where does this new one come from?

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:43.960
It doesn’t appear to come from anywhere,

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:46.360
so we say it’s fictitious or fake.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:47.560
But is it really fake?

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:51.800
Also, why are we treating inertial reference frames like they’re more real than accelerated ones.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:53.960
Shouldn’t everyone be on equal footing either way.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:57.840
These are very good questions and the same ones Einstein was asking himself.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:58.840
Back to the timeline!

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.760
So, 1905, miracle year of physics!

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:05.780
A couple years later, good ‘ol Al started really thinking about acceleration.

00:07:05.789 --> 00:07:11.119
It occurred to him that if you were inside a spaceship accelerating at 22 miles per hour per second

00:07:11.119 --> 00:07:13.919
in a shake-proof sound-proof room with no windows,

00:07:13.919 --> 00:07:17.139
it would feel exactly as if the spaceship was sitting on the ground.

00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:19.020
We call this the equivalence principle.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:22.380
More often than not in physics, if two things are indistinguishable,

00:07:22.380 --> 00:07:25.780
then they are exactly the same thing and it’s not just limited to spaceships.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:28.600
Remember this ball hanging in the accelerating car?

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.400
Yeah, that fake force is actually gravity.

00:07:31.409 --> 00:07:35.439
There’s now a gravity pulling down and another gravity pulling backward.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:39.120
So the total gravity points this way. It kind of defines the new down.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.940
It isn’t any different than if the car was sitting on a hill.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.600
Same exact physics, therefore, same exact thing.

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:46.380
Wait, you said that new force was fake.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:50.420
If it’s actually gravity, doesn't that make the gravity with the Earth is also fake?

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:52.560
Yep! Gravity? Not a real force.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.020
So how does an acceleration in one frame look like a force in another?

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:57.740
For that, back to the timeline!

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:02.740
In 1908, a guy named Hermann Minkowski invented a 4D frame called spacetime.

00:08:02.749 --> 00:08:07.080
Einstein wasn’t a fan but, by 1912, he knew his own approach wasn’t working,

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.740
so he had no choice but to accept Minkowski’s.

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:13.220
The math was a bit beyond him though, so he consulted some math friends

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:17.320
and, in 1915, he finally figured it out after collaborating with David Hilbert.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:19.380
General Relativity was finally a thing.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:24.060
The solution: some accelerated reference frames are inertial reference frames.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:25.800
Before you freak out, let’s review.

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.340
Remember, physics isn’t built on the things that are relative.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:30.600
It’s built on things we call invariants.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:33.220
Those are the things that are the same from all points of view.

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:35.560
Let’s make some lists. I love lists.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:41.180
Relative things: length, distance, time, mass, energy, motion, the list goes on.

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:45.860
Invariant things: the speed of light, spacetime paths, electric charge

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:47.360
and... I think that’s it.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:51.660
In Part 1 of this series, we saw a car moving with steady motion in a straight line.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:53.600
Let’s take a look its spacetime diagram.

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:56.800
As it moves through space, it also moves forward in time.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.300
Represented by this slanted spacetime path.

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:02.260
That path is invariant, so it’s measured the same by everyone,

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:04.560
but, separately, space and time are relative.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:08.700
From the clone’s perspective, he’s sitting
still in space and only moving through time.

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:11.480
On the spacetime diagram, you can’t change the path.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:14.240
It’s invariant, so you have to change the frame itself.

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:18.620
The clone’s time axis lines up with his path, therefore, no moving through space.

00:09:18.620 --> 00:09:22.140
The accelerating clone from this video has to be treated the same way.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:26.280
His spacetime path looks like this, but he also thinks he’s stationary,

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:30.440
so his time axis looks like this. It’s not straight. It’s curved.

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:35.900
Unfortunately, we can’t see the curvature. We always see the frame as straight, even when it isn’t.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:37.620
This makes things look a little weird.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:41.440
And the equivalence principle said accelerations are exactly the same thing as gravity,

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.480
so gravity isn’t a force at all.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:49.360
Gravity is just an illusion created by our perspective. It’s the result of a curved or warped frame.

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:54.080
You think this squirrel is falling toward the Earth speeding up at 22 miles per hour per second?

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:57.920
Nope, it’s moving at steady speed in a straight line through curved spacetime.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:01.160
You think the ISS is orbiting around the Earth in a circle?

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:04.029
Nope, steady speed, straight line, curved spacetime.

00:10:04.029 --> 00:10:06.170
Hopefully, this cleared some things up for many of you

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:09.380
and maybe made your brain hurt a little? It’s good for you every now and then.

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:11.660
If you have any questions, please ask in the comments.

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:14.640
I have a feeling you’re definitely going to need a comment response video for this.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.460
Thanks for liking, sharing, and subscribing.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:19.500
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to be a little crazy.

